NESTING IS WRITTEN AT FOUR COLUMNS A LEVEL, WHATEVER THE SOURCE USED.

Two columns in, four out, at every depth. `then-args` runs `format` again over
the result, so `expected.md` is the SECOND pass: the widening happens once and
then stops.

That "and then stops" is the point. Re-indenting on every pass is the failure
mode this normalization invites, and it is not hypothetical on this code: the
blank line between two items is given to the SHALLOWER of them precisely
because handing it to the deeper one made the parent list loose and gained a
level of indent on every run. A document that drifts four columns per pass
ends up as an indented code block, and prose stops being prose.

Every other list case in this suite writes its fixtures at four columns
already, so none of them can see a change in this behaviour. This one starts
at two on purpose.

WHY WIDENING IS SAFE HERE. Two columns and four columns nest identically under
a `- ` marker, since content begins at column 2 and anything from there to
three past it is item content rather than code. The choice is cosmetic, and
the only thing that could make it a defect is instability, which is what the
second pass rules out.

It is NOT safe in general, and the neighbouring case says where it stops:
`format-reads-indented-content-under-an-empty-item-as-code`. Four columns
under an EMPTY item is measured from the margin, not from any content, and
there it really is code.
